<h2>The framework: why a refund exists at all</h2>
<p>Court fees in Delhi are levied under the Court Fees Act, 1870 as in force in the National Capital Territory — ad valorem on plaints and memoranda of appeal valued for money or property, and at fixed rates for various applications. The fee is the price of invoking the court’s jurisdiction, and the Act’s default position is that it is not returnable merely because the litigation ends early or badly. Refund is the exception, and it is always statutory: a provision must authorise it. The legislature has created those provisions deliberately, and the most important of them reward one thing — <strong>settlement through the court-annexed dispute resolution machinery</strong>.</p>

<h2>Settlement after a Section 89 reference: Section 16 of the Court Fees Act</h2>
<p>Section 89 of the Code of Civil Procedure empowers the court, where elements of a settlement appear to exist, to refer the parties to arbitration, conciliation, judicial settlement including settlement through Lok Adalat, or mediation. Section 16 of the Court Fees Act, 1870 — inserted alongside that scheme — provides that where the court refers the parties under Section 89 and a settlement follows, the plaintiff is entitled to a <strong>certificate from the court authorising refund of the full amount of the fee</strong> paid in respect of the plaint. The logic extends through the appellate stage in the same manner for the fee paid on the memorandum where the appeal is the proceeding referred and settled. The operative requirements are two: a reference by the court under Section 89, and a settlement of the dispute through the mode referred.</p>

<h2>Lok Adalat settlements: Section 21 of the Legal Services Authorities Act</h2>
<p>The Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 contains its own refund command. Under Section 21, every award of a Lok Adalat is deemed to be a decree of a civil court, and where a compromise or settlement is arrived at by a Lok Adalat in a case <em>referred to it</em> by a court, the court fee paid in the case is to be <strong>refunded in the manner provided under the Court Fees Act, 1870</strong>. Two practical notes follow. First, the refund attaches to cases referred from a court and settled; a matter taken directly to a pre-litigation Lok Adalat involves no court fee in the first place, so nothing arises for refund. Second, the Lok Adalat award itself, being final and deemed a decree, is the natural document to record the entitlement, and parties should ask that it do so.</p>

<h2>Other refund situations under the Act</h2>
<p>The 1870 Act contains a small family of older refund provisions worth knowing. Where an appeal succeeds on the ground that the lower court ought to have decided the matter and the case is remanded for retrial, the Act provides for return of the fee paid on the memorandum of appeal. Comparable provisions deal with fees paid on applications for review in defined circumstances. These operate on their own terms and do not depend on settlement. By contrast, the Act contains no general provision refunding the fee simply because a suit is withdrawn; any partial refund on early withdrawal exists only where a specific provision so provides, and counsel should verify the position for the particular court and proceeding before advising a client to expect money back.</p>

<h2>What earns a refund — and what does not</h2>
<table class="law">
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Refund position</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Suit or appeal referred under Section 89 CPC and settled in mediation, conciliation or judicial settlement</td><td>Certificate authorising refund of the full fee paid — Section 16, Court Fees Act, 1870</td></tr>
<tr><td>Case referred by the court to Lok Adalat and settled by award</td><td>Fee refunded in the manner provided under the Court Fees Act — Section 21, Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appeal allowed and case remanded for retrial</td><td>Fee on the memorandum of appeal returnable under the Act’s remand provision</td></tr>
<tr><td>Private compromise recorded without any court reference</td><td>No statutory refund hook; refund ordinarily not available</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dismissal on merits, dismissal for default, or abandonment</td><td>No refund</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>The certificate and the Collector: how the money comes back</h2>
<p>The refund is worked in two stages. The <strong>first stage is before the court</strong>: when the settlement is recorded — the mediated settlement taken on file, the Lok Adalat award passed, the compromise decree drawn after a Section 89 reference — counsel applies for, and the order or decree records, the entitlement to refund and the issue of the refund certificate. It is far easier to have the direction built into the disposal order than to seek it months later. The <strong>second stage is before the revenue machinery</strong>: the certified copy of the order or certificate, with the original proof of payment — the stamped plaint copy or the e-court fee receipt, since fees in Delhi are now commonly paid electronically — is presented through the process the Collector of Stamps follows in Delhi, and the amount is disbursed in accordance with that procedure.</p>

<h2>Practical steps and timing</h2>
<p>A clean refund file is assembled, not improvised: keep the original court fee receipts from the day of filing; at settlement, ask the referring court to record the Section 89 reference and the fact of settlement in the disposal order itself; obtain the certified copy promptly; and lodge the refund claim before the Collector without delay, since revenue refunds are processed under prescribed procedures and timelines and a stale claim invites avoidable objections. Where the fee was paid in parts — on the plaint and later on amendment — reconcile the figures before applying, so the certificate covers the whole of what was actually paid.</p>

<div class="note"><p>The refund is, in substance, a statutory discount on settling through the court’s own machinery. Parties inclined to compromise lose nothing by routing the settlement through a Section 89 reference or a Lok Adalat rather than a purely private compromise — and may recover the entire court fee for the asking.</p></div>
